if you enjoy random short facts about  entertaining events in history you most  certainly came across this famous  did you know post which says that during  the 1866 austro-prussian war  liechtenstein sided with austria and  went to war with its military which was  80 men strong these 80 soldiers saw no  action during this conflict and after it  ended the 80 men returned home with an  extra 81st person who was said to be an  italian friend the soldiers made during  the war  hence liechtenstein's army arrived home  stronger than it departed  this was also the last war liechtenstein  participated in as it disbanded its army  in 1868 and announced permanent  neutrality  then on now is this story true well if  you have seen any of my other  well actually videos you should know by  now that oftentimes these things are  based in truth but not  entirely true so let's see where this  one goes wrong  in the latter half of the 19th century  liechtenstein was ruled by johann ii  prince of liechtenstein he was a fairly  liberal ruler for his time  and in 1862 even issued liechtenstein's  first constitution  however also during this time he along  with the entire liechtenstein's upper  class had very close ties to the  austrian empire meaning when the  austro-prussian war started in 1866  the liechtenstein upper class was let's  say  very strongly persuaded to join austria  this didn't sit all too well with the  liechtenstein people who with their new  constitutional government  heavily voiced their concerns like costs  of going to war or the fact that they  didn't want to fight against fellow  germans or simply that no  liechtensteiner really felt like dying  in a war would be a pleasant experience  etc so the prince being pressured by  austria on one side and by his subjects  on the other  had to do some clever political  maneuvering first he assured the  government that he will take on  all the costs of the military campaign  except the mobilization  second he told his subjects that no  liechtensteiner will have to fight  against fellow germans as the  liechtenstein army would be sent against  the italians who were supporting the  prussians in the war  and third the military was technically  under the direct control of the prince  so in the end  under the premise of defending  liechtenstein against the plausible  invading italian army he went around the  liechtenstein's government and sent the  liechtenstein military to guard the  tyron mountain passes with the austrian  tyra  infantry regiments this way  liechtenstein was practically helping  out austria making them happy but at the  same time as stated before the premise  of liechtenstein's military action was  officially defensive meaning they  technically never declared any war  making the liechtensteiners happy at  least  somewhat this combined with the fact  that over the course of the six weeks  from the liechtenstein's army departure  in july to the end of the war in  september the liechtenstein army never  actively fought anyone  means that as you can see on this map  liechtenstein is officially considered  as being neutral during the war  it was never even mentioned in the peace  treaty because despite their support of  austria liechtenstein was never  considered by prussia as being at war  with them  so was the 1866 austro-prussian war the  last war lichtenstein was in  officially no but technically yes i  guess  okay okay but what of the soldiers were  there truly just 80 soldiers that  guarded the tiro pass  well the lichtestein military at the  time had 82 men and 18 in reserve  however according to this one source two  men stayed behind to guard the barracks  and vadut's castle  plus considering that the reserves most  likely wouldn't go to tiro  means that actually just 80 men  participated in the war  so that part is correct lastly did the  liechtenstein soldiers actually come  back with an italian friend  well considering they went to tyrol to  defend against the italians it would be  very unlikely and in fact it didn't  happen  however that's not to say the soldiers  didn't come back with an extra person  they did a lieutenant of the kaiseriager  an austrian terror infantry regiment  accompanied the liechtenstein soldier's  home as a military liaison  which would make sense because as  mentioned before the liechtenstein's  army cooperated with the turo infantry  regiment during the war  whether this austrian lieutenant was  their friend i would like to think so  but he most likely returned back home to  austria after all was said and done  also the state administrator carl house  won hausen and the advisor to the state  and the prince clemens graffon westfalen  met up with the army on the  liechtenstein border and entered  liechtenstein with them  so technically the liechtenstein army  returned with three extra men  however these two were lichtensteiners  or at least people who worked for  liechtenstein just doing their job so i  wouldn't really count it  in the end liechtenstein participated in  the austro-prussian war  kind of it had a military of 80 men kind  of  it returned with an extra friend kind of  and  two years after in 1868 it disbanded its  army and declared permanent neutrality  henceforth  so the austro-prussian war was the last  liechtenstein war  kind of and that is the true story of  what happened with the liechtenstein  military in  1866. i would like to give a shout out  to the liechtenstein state library and  their amazing online archives without it  this video wouldn't be possible they are  such a small country and their online  archives are better than most countries  and if you want to see more history  videos on here subscribe click the bell  icon and stick around for history
 

